\v 19 So¦124087 the two¦124088 of them travelled on until¦124089 they reached Beyt-Lehem \add !(Bethlehem)\add*. When¦124087 they entered Beyt-Lehem, the whole¦124099 town¦124101 was curious about¦124088 them, and¦124087 the women began asking, “Is¦124104 that¦124104 Naomi¦124105?”
\p
\v 20 But¦124107 Naomi told them¦124108 not¦124109 to call her ‘Naomi’ (which means ‘pleasant’) but¦124107 to call her ‘Mara¦124116’ (meaning ‘miserable’) because¦124117 she said¦124107, “The¦124120 almighty¦124120 \add God\add* has¦124119 made my life very miserable.
\v 21 I went¦124126 away content \add with a family\add*, but¦124127 Yahweh has brought me back with a void. So why call¦124131 me ‘Naomi¦124133’? You see, Yahweh has testified¦124135 against¦124136 me—the powerful¦124137 \add God\add* has brought misfortune on¦124136 me.”
\s1 Ruth collects grain in Boaz' field
\p
\v 22 And¦124145 so Naomi¦124144 had returned¦124143 \add to Yisrael (Israel)\add*, along with¦124156 her¦124147 daughter-in-law¦124147, Ruth¦124145 the Moabitess, and¦124143 they arrived there in Beyt¦124154-Lehem at¦124156 the start of¦124156 the barley¦124158 harvest¦124157.
\c 2
