\v 5 Then¦124231 he¦124231 asked the foreman of the harvesters¦124237, “Whose¦124232 \add daughter\add* is that¦124240 young¦124239 woman¦124239 \add over there\add*?”
\p
\v 6 “She's a young¦124249 Moabitess,” the¦124243 man replied¦124248. “The¦124247 one who¦124252 came¦124252 with¦124253 Naomi¦124255 when she¦124251 returned¦124252 from¦124256 Moab¦124257.
\v 7 She¦124263 asked me¦124260 \add for permission\add* to follow the harvesters¦124266 and pick up any heads of grain that¦124270 drop, and she's been doing it since¦124269 early morning¦124270 until just recently she's taking a break now¦124273 in¦124264 the shelter¦124276.
\p
\v 8 So¦124279 Boaz¦124280 \add approached\add* Ruth¦124283, “Young woman,” he¦124279 said¦124279, “listen here. Don't¦124284 leave this place or go¦124289 to any other field¦124291 to pick up grain, but¦124279 just stay¦124298 close by these young¦124301 female¦124301 workers of¦124301 mine.
\v 9 Watch where \add the men\add* are harvesting and¦124308 follow along behind them. I've told them not to bother you. And whenever you're thirsty, help yourself from the water jars that¦124305 the men keep filled.”
\p
\v 10 Then¦124330 she knelt down¦124334 on¦124341 the ground and bowed¦124334 her¦124333 head low to the ground and said¦124336, “Why are you being so kind to me¦124342 when I'm¦124343 a foreigner¦124347? I wasn't expecting your¦124341 attention.”
\p
\v 11 “People have¦124359 told me¦124355,” Boaz¦124350 replied¦124351, “about¦124362 everything you've¦124359 done¦124359 for your mother-in-law¦124362 since your husband¦124365 died—how you left¦124366 your parents¦124371 and your homeland behind and came¦124374 here to¦124375 live with people¦124377 that¦124378 you hadn't even met before.
\v 12 May Yahweh reward your¦124389 \add hard\add* work¦124387. Since you've¦124397 come¦124397 \add here\add* for protection under¦124399 Yisrael's¦124394 god¦124393 Yahweh, may he reward you in full.”
\p
\v 13 “My lord,” she replied¦124403, “I trust that I'll continue to please you because¦124411 you've¦124412 comforted¦124410 me by speaking¦124412 so kindly to me, your servant, even though I'm¦124404 not¦124418 like¦124420 your servant girls.”
\p
\v 14 Then¦124423 when it was time¦124426 to eat, Boaz¦124425 told Ruth, “Come and join us. Take some bread¦124433 and dip¦124434 it in¦124436 the sauce and eat.” So¦124423 she sat¦124437 beside the harvesters¦124439, and he¦124440 \add also\add* offered¦124440 her¦124424 some roasted¦124443 grain¦124443. She¦124444 ate¦124444 until she was full and \add even\add* had¦124446 some left over.
\v 15 Then¦124448 she¦124459 went back to¦124449 picking up heads and Boaz¦124451 ordered¦124450 his¦124454 men¦124454, “Let¦124452 her gather grain even among the¦124458 bundles and don't¦124460 shame her.
\v 16 Even pull some \add stalks\add* out from the¦124470 bundles¦124470 and drop them¦124471 for¦124467 her to pick up, and don't¦124473 tell her off.”
\p
\v 17 So¦124478 \add Ruth\add* picked up grain in¦124479 the countryside¦124479 until¦124480 evening¦124482 and when she beat¦124483 the heads and collected the grains, she had¦124487 a large basket of barley¦124490.
\v 18 So¦124492 she picked¦124492 up¦124492 the basket and headed back into town¦124494 and showed her mother-in-law¦124496 how much she had¦124508 collected. Ruth also gave¦124502 Naomi the roasted grain left-over from¦124509 her lunch.
\v 19 “Where¦124514 did you pick up grain today and¦124511 where¦124517 did you work¦124518?” asked Naomi. “May¦124519 the¦124520 man¦124531 who¦124520 took notice¦124520 of¦124530 you be blessed¦124521.”
\p So¦124511 she told¦124522 her¦124513 mother-in-law¦124513 where she had¦124527 worked, “The¦124530 name¦124530 of the man I¦124533 worked with¦124534 today is¦124536 Boaz¦124536.”
