\v 23 So¦124612 Ruth stayed¦124612 near Boaz' servant¦124613 girls and picked up heads of grain until¦124616 the end of the barley¦124621 harvest and \add then\add* the wheat¦124623 harvest, and she¦124624 lived¦124624 with¦124613 her¦124627 mother-in-law¦124627.
\c 3
\s1 Ruth requests Boaz to assume his responsibilities
\rem /s1 Ruth at the Threshing Floor; Ruth and Boaz at the Threshing Floor; Ruth Finds a Husband
\p
\v 1 One day Ruth's mother-in-law¦124632 Naomi¦124631 said¦124629 to her¦124632, “My daughter¦124633, I think I need to be finding a secure living situation for you?
\v 2 Now¦124644, Boaz¦124646 whose¦124652 servant¦124652 girls you've been working with¦124650, he's a relative¦124647 of ours. In¦124653 fact, he'll¦124655 be on the threshing¦124659 floor¦124659 winnowing¦124656 barley¦124660 tonight.
\v 3 So¦124663 wash¦124663 yourself¦124675 and put¦124666 on¦124669 some perfume. Wear your¦124667 cloak and go¦124670 out to the threshing¦124672 floor¦124672, but¦124663 don't¦124673 let him know¦124675 you're there until¦124677 he's¦124678 finished¦124678 eating¦124679 and drinking¦124680.
\v 4 Then when¦124683 he lies down \add to sleep\add*, pay attention to where¦124688 he lies down. \add Once he's asleep,\add* go¦124692 and lift the blanket near his feet¦124694 and lie¦124689 down. Then he'll¦124689 tell¦124698 you what to do¦124702 \add next\add*.”
\p
\v 5 “I'll¦124712 do¦124712 everything you¦124710 say,” Ruth answered¦124705,
\v 6 then¦124714 she went¦124714 out to the threshing¦124715 floor¦124715 and¦124716 did¦124716 everything her¦124721 mother-in-law¦124721 had¦124720 instructed¦124720.
\p
\v 7 When¦124723 Boaz¦124724 had finished eating¦124723 and drinking¦124725 and was¦124726 feeling cheery, he¦124728 went¦124728 off to¦124729 lie¦124729 down at¦124730 the end¦124730 of the pile of grain¦124731. Then¦124723 Ruth quietly¦124733 followed him, and uncovered¦124734 his¦124735 feet¦124735 and lay¦124736 down \add there\add*.
\v 8 But¦124738 Boaz¦124744 was startled¦124741 in¦124739 the middle¦124739 of¦124739 the night¦124740, and¦124738 when he turned¦124743 over he was stunned to find a woman¦124745 lying¦124746 at¦124739 his¦124747 feet¦124747.
\v 9 “Who¦124750 are¦124752 you¦124752?” he¦124749 asked.
\p “I'm¦124755 your servant¦124762 Ruth¦124756,” she¦124754 replied¦124749, “Spread¦124758 your cloak¦124759 over¦124760 me\f + \fr 3:9 \ft This was the cultural way for Ruth to request Boaz to take her as his wife.\f* because¦124763 you have a responsibility as our close relative.”
\p
\v 10 “My dear girl, may Yahweh¦124770 bless¦124768 you.” Boaz replied¦124767, “You¦124772 have¦124772 shown more¦124775 kindness¦124773 now that before,\f + \fr 3:10 \ft Probably referring to Ruth's faithfulness to her mother-in-law.\f* because you haven't chased after¦124781 the young¦124782 men¦124782, whether¦124783 rich or poor.
\v 11 So¦124790 now¦124790 dear girl, don't¦124792 be afraid. I'll¦124799 do what you've asked¦124798 for¦124801, because¦124802 the¦124806 whole¦124795 town knows¦124803 that¦124808 you're¦124798 an honourable woman¦124809.
\v 12 It¦124825 is¦124822 true that I¦124820 have a responsibility towards your family,\x + \xo 3:12: \xt Ruth 2:20.\x* but¦124813 there's¦124822 another man who's more¦124825 closely related \add to you all\add* than¦124825 me.
\v 13 Stay here for the rest of the night¦124829, and¦124830 then in¦124831 the morning, if he'll¦124834 accept then he can \add marry\add* you. But¦124830 if he's not willing¦124840 \add to take care of you\add*, then as Yahweh¦124846 lives, I'll¦124842 do it¦124830 myself. Now lie¦124847 down¦124847 here until¦124848 morning.”
\p
\v 14 So¦124852 \add Ruth\add* lay¦124852 \add there\add* near his feet¦124853 until¦124859 morning¦124861, but¦124852 she¦124876 got up before¦124863 it was light enough to be recognised¦124865 as Boaz had said¦124870, “\add It's best\add* if¦124874 no one knows¦124873 that¦124874 a woman¦124877 visited the threshing¦124878 floor¦124878.”
\v 15 Then Boaz said¦124880, “Bring your cloak¦124882 here and hold¦124886 it out.” So¦124880 she held it while he poured a generous amount of barley¦124894 into it, and lifted it onto her \add shoulder\add*. Then \add ?he or she\add*\f + \fr 3:15 \ft TC: There's no separate Hebrew word for ‘he’ or ‘she’ here—it's part of the verb ‘he/she_went’ but it's unclear if this original verb was marked \+it masculine\+it* or \+it feminine\+it*, hence you'll find both alternatives in various Bible translations.\f* went¦124897 into the¦124898 town¦124898.
\v 16 When¦124900 Ruth got home, her mother-in-law¦124903 asked her, “My daughter¦124908, \add how did everything go\add*?”
\p So¦124900 Ruth told¦124909 her everything that¦124915 Boaz \add had said and\add* done¦124916 for¦124918 her
\v 17 and¦124921 she¦124921 also said¦124921, “He¦124928 gave¦124926 me all this barley¦124924 because¦124928 he told me that he didn't¦124931 want me to return to my mother-in-law¦124937 empty-handed¦124934.”
\p
\v 18 “\add Okay\add* my daughter¦124941,” Naomi responded¦124939. “Wait \add now\add* until we see what happens, because¦124948 that¦124943 man¦124951 won't¦124949 rest today until the matter¦124947 gets settled.”
\c 4
\s1 Boaz takes over the land and gets Ruth as a wife
\rem /s1 Boaz Marries Ruth; The Marriage of Boaz and Ruth
\p
\v 1 \add Meanwhile,\add* Boaz went¦124961 to \add the place inside\add* the town gate¦124962 \add (where people conducted their official business)\add* and sat down there¦124964. Just as he had hoped, the man responsible for Naomi's family came along—the man whom¦124968 Boaz had mentioned earlier—and Boaz called out to him by name and said¦124972, “Come over here¦124976 and sit¦124974 down.” So¦124960 the man came over and sat down.
\v 2 Then¦124982 Boaz called over ten¦124983 of the older, well-respected men¦124984 from the town¦124986 and asked them to sit¦124988 \add and witness their discussion\add*, so they¦124991 sat¦124991 down¦124991 \add with them\add*.
\v 3 Then¦124993 Boaz asked the other relative, “The part of the countryside¦124996 that¦124997 belonged¦124998 to our¦124998 relative Elimelek¦124999 is for sale. Naomi¦125001, who¦125002 \add recently\add* returned¦125002 from¦125003 Moab¦125004, is selling¦125000 it.
\v 4 I thought that I should tell¦125025 you \add about it\add* and \add in case\add* you buy \add the land\add* while the people sitting¦125013 here are listening, including¦125014 these leaders of our people. If¦125017 you want to buy it back into the family, then do so. But¦125006 if you do not want to buy it back, then let me know¦125027, because¦125029 you are the closest relative to Elimelek, and I am¦125034 next after¦125034 you.”
\p “I'll¦125008 buy it¦125035,” the man replied¦125007.
\p
\v 5 “When¦125039 you buy the land from Naomi¦125046,” Boaz¦125040 continued¦125039, “you will also need to¦125055 marry Ruth¦125048, our deceased relative's widow from Moab, so that she'll have¦125053 a son who will inherit¦125061 the property and¦125039 continue Elimelek's family line.”
\p
\v 6 “I can't buy it back myself, then¦125063,” the closer relative answered¦125063. “If I did that, I would ruin¦125073 my¦125076 own¦125076 son's inheritance¦125076. You¦125080 can have the land and¦125063 the woman instead of me, because¦125084 I \add simply\add* can't take it.”
\p
\v 7 (At that¦125090 time in Yisrael, people had a different way of buying \add land\add* or exchanging¦125098 \add goods between them\add*. In order to make these matters¦125102 legally binding, one man would take off¦125103 one of his¦125105 sandals¦125105 and¦125106 give¦125106 it¦125106 to the other¦125107 party. That was¦125109 the way people in Yisrael confirmed¦125099 what they had agreed to do.)\x + \xo 4:7-8: \xt Deu 25:9.\x*
\p
\v 8 “You buy it yourself,” the¦125113 relative said¦125112 and he¦125118 took off¦125118 one of¦125119 his¦125119 sandals¦125119 \add and gave it to Boaz\add*.
\v 9 Then¦125121 Boaz¦125122 said¦125121 to the respected men and to all the other people¦125126 who were there, “Today you¦125128 have all witnessed¦125127 that I've bought from Naomi¦125145 all the property that belonged¦125142 to Elimelek¦125137, Kilion¦125142, and Mahlon¦125143.
\v 10 I¦125154 am also¦125147 taking Ruth¦125150, Mahlon's¦125153 widow from Moab, to be my wife¦125152 so that she can have a son. Everyone will consider that son to be Elimelek's descendant and he will inherit¦125163 the¦125158 property and carry on Elimelek's family name¦125158 among¦125170 his relatives¦125171 and here in his hometown.\x + \xo 4:10: \xt Deu 25:5-6.\x* You've seen and heard that today \add and can tell anyone who asks about it\add*.”
\p
\v 11 All¦125179 the respected men, and the others who were sitting at¦125184 the town gate¦125184, agreed, and they said¦125178, “Yes, \add we are your\add*. We pray that¦125182 Yahweh¦125188 will allow this woman¦125191 who will be coming¦125192 into¦125193 your home, to be like¦125196 Rachel¦125196 and Leah¦125198, the two¦125201 who gave birth to our ancestors and started our people¦125181, Yisrael¦125205.\x + \xo 4:11: \xt Gen 29:31.\x* May you become great in the clan of Efratah¦125209 and famous here in Beyt-Lehem!
\v 12 We pray that your family will be like the family of your ancestor Perez¦125219, son of Judah and Tamar¦125223, because of the many descendants that Yahweh¦125230 will give¦125229 to¦125231 you and this¦125235 young¦125234 woman¦125234.”\x + \xo 4:12: \xt Gen 38:27-30.\x*
\s1 Boaz' descendants
\rem /s1 The Genealogy of David; The Descendants of Boaz; Boaz and His Descendants
\p
\v 13 So¦125237 Boaz¦125238 married Ruth¦125241, and \add when\add* he¦125246 \add ≈slept with\add* her, Yahweh¦125249 enabled her to become¦125242 pregnant and she¦125252 gave¦125252 birth¦125252 to a son¦125253.
\v 14 The¦125256 women¦125256 \add in Beyt-Lehem\add* said¦125255 to Naomi¦125259, “Praise Yahweh for giving you a \add male descendant\add* to preserve your family today. \add We pray to Yahweh that people throughout\add* Yisrael¦125270 will greatly respect him.
\v 15 Your daughter-in-law¦125281, who loves¦125284 you and who's been better to you than if you had seven¦125291 sons¦125292, has¦125285 given¦125285 birth¦125285 to him¦125285. Now he'll make you feel young again and he'll take care of you when you get old¦125279.”
\v 16 Then¦125294 Naomi¦125295 picked up the¦125298 baby and held him closely and became¦125301 like second mother to¦125303 him.
\p
\v 17 The women who were living nearby said¦125310, “It is as if Naomi¦125314 now has¦125311 a son¦125313!” They¦125306 named him¦125307 Obed¦125317.
\rem /s1 The ancestry of David
\p \add Eventually\add* Obed went on to become the father of Yishay¦125321 \add !(Jesse¦125321)\add*, who became the father of \add King\add* David¦125323.
\v 18 Here is a list of the¦125327 descendants¦125327 of Perez: Perez's¦125328 son was Hezron¦125333.
\v 19 Hezron's son was Ram and¦125340 Ram's¦125339 son was Amminadab¦125344.
\v 20 Amminadab's son was Nahshon and¦125351 Nahshon's¦125350 son was Salmon.
\v 21 Salmon's son was Boaz and¦125362 Boaz's¦125361 son was Obed¦125366.
\v 22 Obed's son was Yishay \add !(Jesse)\add* and¦125373 Yishay's¦125372 son was King David¦125377.
